{
  "_comment": [
    "One corpus, two validators. core/chat_codec.protocol_of (python) and",
    "ChatProtocol.parseProtocol (chat-protocol.js) must agree on every case",
    "here, because they gate the SAME bus from opposite ends: python decides",
    "what a client is allowed to SEND and what it is handed on RECEIVE, and the",
    "JS decides what the client acts on. A rule in one and not the other means",
    "either a client builds a payload its own validator blessed and gets a 400,",
    "or an inbound carrier silently never reaches the bus.",
    "",
    "That is not hypothetical: python capped number magnitude at 1e15 and the JS",
    "only checked isFinite, so anything from 1e15 up disagreed. This file exists",
    "so the next divergence fails a test instead of desyncing a room.",
    "",
    "Add a case here whenever either validator learns a new rule."
  ],
  "accept": [
    {
      "why": "the ordinary case",
      "p": {
        "k": "jbx.vote",
        "o": "abc123"
      }
    },
    {
      "why": "dotted kind",
      "p": {
        "k": "poll.cast",
        "o": "yes"
      }
    },
    {
      "why": "bare kind, no dot",
      "p": {
        "k": "ping"
      }
    },
    {
      "why": "kind at the 24 char limit",
      "p": {
        "k": "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "why": "digits are allowed after the first letter",
      "p": {
        "k": "c4.move2"
      }
    },
    {
      "why": "booleans and null are scalars",
      "p": {
        "k": "x",
        "a": true,
        "b": false,
        "c": null
      }
    },
    {
      "why": "a millisecond timestamp is comfortably under the cap",
      "p": {
        "k": "x",
        "ts": 1788468529000
      }
    },
    {
      "why": "negative numbers count by magnitude",
      "p": {
        "k": "x",
        "n": -1000
      }
    },
    {
      "why": "floats are fine",
